91\u00b0, 4.6 ft (1.41 m); 12.5 s from WNW 282\u00b0, 2.8 ft (0.86 m). Breaking at 5:00 AM, against 11:00 AM yesterday: North 4.6 ft (1.41 m), little change; Center 4.4 ft (1.34 m), filling from 3.6 ft (1.11 m); South 3.9 ft (1.19 m), little change. North leads, as it did."],["Tide","The tide is pushing to 5.5 ft (1.67 m) at 6:58 AM then dropping to 3.9 ft (1.18 m) at 12:18 PM. The previous AM low was 2.0 ft (0.62 m) at 12:08 AM and the PM high will be 5.7 ft (1.75 m) at 5:55 PM. The gauge is running 1.3 ft (0.40 m) over the harmonic prediction, over the last 3 days. Today's range is 3.4 ft (1.05 m), neap tides; springs around Oct 25. On the 2016 survey the tide moves where the waves break, not how big they are; this season's sandbars are not known."],["Wind","Calm at 5:52 AM. At this hour on the 29 days before: North light or variable on 11, offshore on 13; Center light or variable on 11, offshore on 12; South light or variable on 11, offshore on 13. Median 5 mph (4 kt)."],["Watching","Former hurricane [Trudy](https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/archive/2026/TRUDY_graphics.php): every fix NHC has logged or forecast is behind land from Point Loma South, so nothing it has sent reaches the breaks."]]}}